const CASE_DATA = {"CASE-01":{"case":{"caseId":"CASE-01","caseNumber":1,"title":"2025 SAP Case – When ERP Transformation Stalls","authoritativeCaseContent":"Case #1. 2025 SAP Case – When ERP Transformation Stalls\n\nCase Summary\nSAP is materially behind its own cloud transition plan. The company expected a steady decline in on-premise support revenue as customers migrated to cloud ERP, targeting a 26% drop from about €11.5B (2021) to €8.5B (2025). Instead, 2025 on-prem support remains at €10.5B, only 7% down from €11.29B in 2024, leaving SAP roughly €2B off its transition expectations.\nThe root cause is slow customer migration from ECC to S/4HANA. Despite looming deadlines - 2027 (mainstream support end) and 2030 (extended support, +2% premium) - a large portion of the installed base is not moving. Estimates suggest 10,000+ customers may still rely on ECC by 2033, particularly complex enterprises.\nIn response, SAP is reframing its commercial strategy. Instead of relying on large-scale ERP transformation as the primary growth engine, it is shifting toward AI-driven value, consumption-based pricing, and incremental upsell across its installed base. Internally, this includes elevating customer value realization to board level and building new structures to drive adoption and monetization of AI capabilities.\nhttps://www.theregister.com/2026/03/24/sap_commercial_focus/","studentQuestions":["Migrate now or delay?","AI = value or revenue recovery?","How to control AI consumption costs?"],"relatedTopics":["Software Inflation","SaaS Market Reality & Trends","Digitalization Paradox","TCO of a Technology Service","SaaS/AI Commercial Strategies and Industry Trends"],"embeddedCourseGuidance":"Case #1: 2025 SAP Case – When ERP Transformation Stalls\n\nCase Summary\nSAP is materially behind its own cloud transition plan. The company expected a steady decline in on-premise support revenue as customers migrated to cloud ERP, targeting a 26% drop from about €11.5B (2021) to €8.5B (2025). Instead, 2025 on-prem support remains at €10.5B, only 7% down from €11.29B in 2024, leaving SAP roughly €2B off its transition expectations.\nThe root cause is slow customer migration from ECC to S/4HANA. Despite looming deadlines - 2027 (mainstream support end) and 2030 (extended support, +2% premium) - a large portion of the installed base is not moving. Estimates suggest 10,000+ customers may still rely on ECC by 2033, particularly complex enterprises.\nIn response, SAP is reframing its commercial strategy. Instead of relying on large-scale ERP transformation as the primary growth engine, it is shifting toward AI-driven value, consumption-based pricing, and incremental upsell across its installed base. Internally, this includes elevating customer value realization to board level and building new structures to drive adoption and monetization of AI capabilities.\nhttps://www.theregister.com/2026/03/24/sap_commercial_focus/\n\nDiscussion\nMigrate now or delay?\nAI = value or revenue recovery?\nHow to control AI consumption costs?\n\nDon’t accept bundled value narratives (separate core ERP value from AI) \nContain AI cost (measure adoption, allow scale-down, require transparent pricing logic)\nPhase adoption (don’t buy promised value)\nThe deadline pressure is on the vendor’s side. Don’t allow to shift it onto you.","source":{"caseDeck":"Training Cases (June 2026).pptx","caseSlide":2,"courseDeck":"CIPS Technology IT Procurement Foundations Slides July 2026 (With Menti and Additional Slides).pptx","courseSlide":13}},"knowledge":{"slide-008":{"slideNumber":8,"content":"The progressive growth of an organization’s software estate—in terms of applications, subscriptions, licenses, and consumption-based services—leading to rising costs, increasing complexity, and diminishing cost efficiency.\n\nSoftware Inflation\n\nShelfware\n\nThe software or digital licenses that an organization pays for but never actively installs, uses, or fully adopts.\n\nSoftware spend spreads from 4% to 18% of an average company's revenue, depending on the industry."},"slide-010":{"slideNumber":10,"content":"2025-2026 SaaS Market trends and challenges\n\nhttps://newsroom.taylorandfrancisgroup.com/costly-business-overhauls-are-not-needed-to-embrace-new-digital-technologies-according-to-specialist/\n© Zylo's 2026 SaaS Management Index \nhttps://www.vertice.one/insights/saas-inflation-rate\nhttps://www.vertice.one/reports/beyond-the-numbers-report-thank-you  \nhttps://www.highalpha.com/saas-benchmarks \nhttps://softwareequity.com/research/annual-saas-report\nhttps://aventis-advisors.com/saas-valuation-multiples/\nhttps://www.vertice.one/reports/beyond-the-numbers-report-thank-you\nhttps://www.vertice.one/l/saas-inflation-index-report\nhttps://www.statista.com/outlook/tmo/public-cloud/software-as-a-service/worldwide\nhttps://www.forbes.com/sites/steveandriole/2021/03/25/3-main-reasons-why-big-technology-projects-fail---why-many-companies-should-just-never-do-them/\nhttps://productiv.com/state-of-saas/ \nhttps://www.fortunebusinessinsights.com/digital-transformation-market-104878 \t\nhttps://www.saas-capital.com/research/private-saas-company-growth-rate-benchmarks/ \nhttps://www.marketsandmarkets.com/Market-Reports/artificial-intelligence-ai-as-a-service-market-121842268.html\n\n6 new apps per month (100 days to purchase)\n4.7 renewals per month (60 days to renew).\n77% of contracts are for one year only.\nSaaS prices rise on average by 9% when auto-renewed.\n\nIn 2025, the average SaaS spend per employee was $4830 (+21% YoY), with 275 apps per company. \n36-50% SaaS licenses remain  unused or underused against industry-recommended levels (SHELFWARE)\nOn average, 23 apps (8% of total) incur multichannel spend.\n7 redundant apps per function (e.g., AI apps, online training, project management, team collaboration)\n\nSoftware-as-a-Service market reached $316 Bn in 2025 (from $266 Bn in 2024, +19% YoY). It should hit US$1.5T by 2034.\nThe AIaaS market in 2025 was $20.3 Bn and is projected to reach $91.2 Bn by 2030 (35% YoY).\n\nAccording to Fortune, global spending on digital transformation will reach $3.1 trillion in 2026. However, roughly 70% of these fail to meet their goals (Gartner).\n\n33% of SaaS apps remain shadow IT. 60% of builders have built something outside IT oversight in the past year.\nChatGPT was the most expensed IT application in 2025. \n3.4% (down from 7%)  of employees expense the software cost. This represents 3.7% of spend (up from 1%).\n\n58% of SaaS vendors hiked prices in 2025, 79% of customers noticed a price increase, while 77% - unexpected post-negotiation cost\n\n57% of SaaS vendors hide their pricing from public view\n\nSoftware inflation surged to 13.2% in March 2026, more than 2% from March 2025 and nearly 10% above the US CPI of 3.3%.\n\nIn 2025, the median EBITDA margin for the SaaS industry reached 9%, with a 26% growth rate (Rule of 40*). The median net profit margin remained at 1.5%-2%, while gross margins were around 80%.\n\nIT owned 26% of app spend and 16% apps in 2025.\nThe business owned 70% of the spend and 51% apps.\n\nThe cost of fighting global poverty is $324 billion.\n\n50% of SaaS companies included AI features in their portfolios in 2025.\nSpending on AI-native apps (AI as a core functionality) surged 75% YoY.\n\nBy 2027, 70% of the top 20 SaaS vendors by revenue will offer consumption-based pricing for a portion of their product portfolio.\n\nEBITDA + Growth ≥40% = healthy balance of growth and profitability"},"slide-011":{"slideNumber":11,"content":"What are your key conclusions/takeaways from the SaaS market overview?"},"slide-012":{"slideNumber":12,"content":"1. SaaS Cost and Governance Management\nSaaS waste (shelfware, redundant apps, and multichannel spend) remains material\nShadow IT and decentralised IT demand undermine control, while Shadow IT shifts towards vibe coding.\nMassive digital investments require strict ROI discipline\n\n2. SaaS Vendors’ Drive to Improve Margins and EBITDA\nSaaS inflation and auto-renewals\nChanges in licensing models\nUnexpected costs and bill shock\n\n3. Shift to AI-Native SaaS Models and PaaS-centric Business Models\nAI app and feature spend skyrockets\nIn the face of vibe coding, there’s a shift from end-user solutions to service orchestration and integration (TBD later) \nAI functionality is becoming the decisive factor for the supplier/solution selection\nIT procurement skills, practice, and governance in the AI sub-category are still being established"},"slide-013":{"slideNumber":13,"content":"Case #1: 2025 SAP Case – When ERP Transformation Stalls\n\nCase Summary\nSAP is materially behind its own cloud transition plan. The company expected a steady decline in on-premise support revenue as customers migrated to cloud ERP, targeting a 26% drop from about €11.5B (2021) to €8.5B (2025). Instead, 2025 on-prem support remains at €10.5B, only 7% down from €11.29B in 2024, leaving SAP roughly €2B off its transition expectations.\nThe root cause is slow customer migration from ECC to S/4HANA. Despite looming deadlines - 2027 (mainstream support end) and 2030 (extended support, +2% premium) - a large portion of the installed base is not moving. Estimates suggest 10,000+ customers may still rely on ECC by 2033, particularly complex enterprises.\nIn response, SAP is reframing its commercial strategy. Instead of relying on large-scale ERP transformation as the primary growth engine, it is shifting toward AI-driven value, consumption-based pricing, and incremental upsell across its installed base. Internally, this includes elevating customer value realization to board level and building new structures to drive adoption and monetization of AI capabilities.\nhttps://www.theregister.com/2026/03/24/sap_commercial_focus/\n\nDiscussion\nMigrate now or delay?\nAI = value or revenue recovery?\nHow to control AI consumption costs?\n\nDon’t accept bundled value narratives (separate core ERP value from AI) \nContain AI cost (measure adoption, allow scale-down, require transparent pricing logic)\nPhase adoption (don’t buy promised value)\nThe deadline pressure is on the vendor’s side. Don’t allow to shift it onto you."},"slide-018":{"slideNumber":18,"content":"“…the use of digital technologies to innovate a business model and provide new revenue streams and value-producing opportunities in industrial ecosystems…”\n\nSource: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0040162523008454#bb0220\n\nWhile digitization focuses on converting and recording data, digitalization is all about developing processes and changing workflows to improve manual systems.\n\nSource: https://www.globalsign.com/en-sg/blog/difference-and-similarities-digitization-digitalization-and-digital-transformation#:~:text=While%20digitization%20focuses%20on%20converting,generate%20insights%20from%20their%20behaviour"},"slide-019":{"slideNumber":19,"content":"The process of converting information or physical objects into a digital format.\n\nDigitization\n\nDigitalization\n\nThe use of digital technologies to transform existing business processes, services, or products."},"slide-067":{"slideNumber":67,"content":"SaaS/AI Commercial Strategies and Industry Trends\n\nConsumption-based, value-based, or hybrid pricing"},"slide-073":{"slideNumber":73,"content":"SaaS Market Challenges and Trends\n\nKey SaaS Market Challenges:\nCustomer demands for flexibility and personalization\nDifficulty in predicting long-term subscription revenue\nUnderutilization of product features leading to churn\nCompetitive pricing pressures and service differentiation\nNeed to monetize AI\nCustomers struggling to select a suitable pricing model\n\nhttps://www.zuora.com/resource/how-consumption-models-contribute-to-business-success/\n\n…directly tie the value of our offering to a clear, transparent consumption metric that makes sense for both customers and vendors… © Bain"},"slide-075":{"slideNumber":75,"content":"How to contain the token waste?\n\nUsage governance (who uses and how that usage is capped)\nUser education, e.g., \n“politeness”, \nexcessive prompts -> knowledge files,\nlong conversations (context window)\nPatches vs. rewrites \nOCR resolution, etc.\n3. LLM Model selection for the task (deep research vs simple tasks) -> AI Gateway\n4. Corporate prompt library (standard questions) and cached responses (standard answers).\n5. Deterministic rules and workflows. \n6. Agent management (calls, communication, retries)\n7. Purposeful knowledge files and operating instructions."},"slide-076":{"slideNumber":76,"content":"https://help.sap.com/doc/sap-api-policy/latest/en-US/API_Policy_latest.pdf"}}},"CASE-02":{"case":{"caseId":"CASE-02","caseNumber":2,"title":"Opportunity Cost & TCO (British Airways IT Outsourcing)","authoritativeCaseContent":"The fundamental economic problem is scarcity. Wants are unlimited, and resources are finite, so choices must be made. Resources must be used and distributed optimally. This gives rise to opportunity cost. The opportunity cost of a choice is the value of the next best alternative forgone. \nIn 2016, British Airways outsourced its UK-based IT staff (over 700 people) as part of its business strategy. \nThis outsourcing strategy allegedly resulted in a 2017 global flight disruption that affected 75,000 passengers and caused a nearly £150 million loss.\nPreviously, BA claimed a 25% reduction in its IT operational costs. However, due to some simple considerations, it’s unclear if the outsourcing risk exposure has been accounted for in the business case. \nA typical large company's IT budget (over $1 billion in revenues) is 2% of revenues, translating into approximately £210 million for BA in 2016. Let's assume 50% of that is related to infrastructure operations, maintenance, support, security, etc. A 25% saving corresponds to just £27.5 million vs. the 2017 crisis cost of £150 million.\nLet's assume a business case as follows.\n\nCase #2. Opportunity cost & TCO (British Airways IT Outsourcing)","studentQuestions":["Would you recommend outsourcing or insourcing?","What could be the opportunity cost?","What risks may have been underestimated?"],"relatedTopics":["TCO of a Technology Service","Opportunity Cost","Types of IT Outsourcing Services","IT Service Sourcing Strategy"],"embeddedCourseGuidance":"The fundamental economic problem is scarcity. Wants are unlimited, and resources are finite, so choices must be made. Resources must be used and distributed optimally. This gives rise to opportunity cost. The opportunity cost of a choice is the value of the next best alternative forgone. \nIn 2016, British Airways outsourced its UK-based IT staff (over 700 people) as part of its business strategy. \nThis outsourcing strategy allegedly resulted in a 2017 global flight disruption that affected 75,000 passengers and caused a nearly £150 million loss.\nPreviously, BA claimed a 25% reduction in its IT operational costs. However, due to some simple considerations, it’s unclear if the outsourcing risk exposure has been accounted for in the business case. \nA typical large company's IT budget (over $1 billion in revenues) is 2% of revenues, translating into approximately £210 million for BA in 2016. Let's assume 50% of that is related to infrastructure operations, maintenance, support, security, etc. A 25% saving corresponds to just £27.5 million vs. the 2017 crisis cost of £150 million.\nLet's assume a business case as follows.\n\nOpportunity cost & TCO (British Airways IT Outsourcing)\n\nWhat would you recommend - outsourcing or insourcing?\nWhat could be the opportunity cost?\n\n2017 risk exposure of £150 million makes this business case break even.  \nThe average revenue per employee in the airline industry in 2016 was £350,000 * 700 * 5 years = £1,225 billion.\n\nWith a profit margin of 5.1% in the airline industry in 2016, £1 savings = £ 19.61 in revenue. The opportunity cost is £1,225 billion / £19.61 = £62.5 million.","source":{"caseDeck":"Training Cases (June 2026).pptx","caseSlide":3,"courseDeck":"CIPS Technology IT Procurement Foundations Slides July 2026 (With Menti and Additional Slides).pptx","courseSlide":43}},"knowledge":{"slide-037":{"slideNumber":37,"content":"Source: ITIL® is a (registered) Trademark of AXELOS Limited.\n\nService strategy\n\nService Knowledge Management System (SKMS) \nincluding the Service Portfolio and Service Catalogue\n\nService design\n\nService transition\n\nService operation\n\nREQUIREMENTS\n\nTHE BUSINESS / CUSTOMERS\n\nOperational plans\n\nImprovement actions & plans\n\nTransition plans\n\nSKMS updated\n\nTested solutions\n\nSolution designs\n\nArchitectures\n\nStandards\n\nSDPs\n\nOperational services\n\nStrategies\n\nPolicies\n\nResource and constraints\n\nSLPs from Requirements\n\nContinual service improvement"},"slide-038":{"slideNumber":38,"content":""},"slide-040":{"slideNumber":40,"content":"When does a project end?"},"slide-041":{"slideNumber":41,"content":"Session 2 Integrated Procurement Planning"},"slide-042":{"slideNumber":42,"content":"Cost of discovery (BA)\n\nCost of service design\u000bCost of supplier selection\nMarket research\nReference and background checks\nEvaluation\nPOC/MVP/Prototype\nCommunication\nTravel\nRelationship building\nNegotiations\nCost of contract negotiations\n\nCost of supplier audit\nCost of scoping\nCost of setup (cloud and infra)\nCost of customisation\nCost of perpetual software\nCost of hardware and end-user devices (including logistics)\nCost of deployment (Including travel)\nCost of migration (data and users)\nCost of testing\nCost of legacy platform decommissioning\nCost of project management\nCost of knowledge management\nCost of change management (documents, processes, comms)\n\nSession 2 Integrated Procurement Planning\n\nCost of cloud services\nCost of software rental\nCost of communication network\nCost of change requests (incl renegotiation)\nCost of quality management\nCost of support\nCost of maintenance\n- Preventative and corrective – SaaS\n- Perfective and adaptive –    ad-hoc \nCost of upgrades\n- Software\n- Firmware\nCost of asset management\nCost of SRM (incl. supplier performance management)\nCost of compliance \n- Technical \n- Legal \n- Environmental\nCost of risk management\n\nCost of retirement/\nReplacement (incl environmental costs)"},"slide-043":{"slideNumber":43,"content":"The fundamental economic problem is scarcity. Wants are unlimited, and resources are finite, so choices must be made. Resources must be used and distributed optimally. This gives rise to opportunity cost. The opportunity cost of a choice is the value of the next best alternative forgone. \nIn 2016, British Airways outsourced its UK-based IT staff (over 700 people) as part of its business strategy. \nThis outsourcing strategy allegedly resulted in a 2017 global flight disruption that affected 75,000 passengers and caused a nearly £150 million loss.\nPreviously, BA claimed a 25% reduction in its IT operational costs. However, due to some simple considerations, it’s unclear if the outsourcing risk exposure has been accounted for in the business case. \nA typical large company's IT budget (over $1 billion in revenues) is 2% of revenues, translating into approximately £210 million for BA in 2016. Let's assume 50% of that is related to infrastructure operations, maintenance, support, security, etc. A 25% saving corresponds to just £27.5 million vs. the 2017 crisis cost of £150 million.\nLet's assume a business case as follows.\n\nOpportunity cost & TCO (British Airways IT Outsourcing)\n\nWhat would you recommend - outsourcing or insourcing?\nWhat could be the opportunity cost?\n\n2017 risk exposure of £150 million makes this business case break even.  \nThe average revenue per employee in the airline industry in 2016 was £350,000 * 700 * 5 years = £1,225 billion.\n\nWith a profit margin of 5.1% in the airline industry in 2016, £1 savings = £ 19.61 in revenue. The opportunity cost is £1,225 billion / £19.61 = £62.5 million."},"slide-083":{"slideNumber":83,"content":"Sourcing of IT \u000bServices"},"slide-084":{"slideNumber":84,"content":"Session 4 Sourcing of IT Services\n\nManaged Services\n\nMobilization\n\nOccasional covering of resource gaps\n\nOutsourcing of complete teams to deliver a project or a simple business process\n\nInfrastructure-centric outsourcing of highly complex business processes or critical services\n\nQuick\n\nSlow\n\nSlowest\n\nContract\n\nHighest\n\nNormal\n\nLow\n\nHighest\n\nNormal\n\nHigh\n\nIT Outsourcing\n\nStaff Augmentation\n\nAs-a-Service\n\nControl\n\nRate card / T&M\n\nProject fee or retainer\n\nSLA-based long-term contract with incentives, possibly with the transfer of skills\n\nApplication-centric automated standard tasks in the cloud\n\nImmediate\n\nLowest\n\nLowest\n\nBoiler-plate subscription agreement\n\nCost\n\nUse case\n\nSource of the competitive advantage\nConfidential information and customer data\nCorporate strategy and decision-making.\nRisk management and compliance \nContract negotiation and key legal reviews\n\nCustomer & stakeholder relationships\nHigh-value customer management \nR&D and product design\nCritical data analytics\nPerformance governance (incl. outsourcing management)\n\nWhat critical activities would you NOT outsource?"},"slide-085":{"slideNumber":85,"content":"Session 4 Sourcing of IT Services\n\nService category\n\nInsourced\n\nManaged Services\n\nStaff Augmentation\n\nSelective Sourcing\n\naaS\n\nTypical observations\n\nIT Strategy, Governance & \nStakeholder Management\n\nMission-critical roles essential to organizations ability to delivery quality IT service will always be insourced\n\nIT Support & End-User \u000bEnablement\n\nCommodity services available in market at greater maturity than IT requirements are typically outsourced\n\nEnterprise & Solution \nArchitecture\n\nEnterprise Architecture is retained as a mission-critical strategic function for the IT organization\n\nApplication Management\nEnterprise Applications\n\nValue-add corporate platforms and apps are insource, automation & cloud adoption used for outsource\n\nApplication Management\nBusiness Applications\n\nRetain critical business skills and minimal team for agility in development, outsource day-to-day management\n\nInfrastructure Operations\n\nCommodity services available in market at greater maturity than IT requirements are typically outsourced\n\nProject Execution\n\nRetain PMO & PM team for business agility and expertise, leverage partners for on-demand capacity\n\nInnovation Management\n\nRetain core capability and leverage ecosystem for idea generation and agile execution"}}},"CASE-03":{"case":{"caseId":"CASE-03","caseNumber":3,"title":"Dropbox's Strategic Shift from AWS","authoritativeCaseContent":"Case #3: Dropbox's Strategic Shift from AWS (2016)\n\nBackground: Dropbox, a leader in cloud storage services, initially relied heavily on Amazon Web Services (AWS) to manage its massive data storage needs, handling over 500 petabytes of data (1 Petabyte = 1024 Terabytes). As the company grew, reaching 500 million users by 2016, the need for a tailored, cost-effective solution became increasingly apparent.\nProblem: While AWS provided exceptional scalability and reliability, Dropbox faced increasing costs and a desire for greater control over its infrastructure to optimize performance and enhance security.\nResolution: In 2016, Dropbox undertook a bold move by transitioning 90% of its operations from AWS to its custom-built infrastructure known as \"Magic Pocket.\" This transition involved extensive planning and negotiation with various hardware vendors to procure the necessary equipment at competitive rates.","studentQuestions":["What changed in terms of cloud services?","What changed in terms of cost recognition?","What is the generic name of this move?"],"relatedTopics":["Cloud Deployment Models","Cloud Services","Buy or Build","TCO of a Technology Service","Kraljic Matrix"],"embeddedCourseGuidance":"Dropbox's Strategic Shift from AWS (2016)\nBackground: Dropbox, a leader in cloud storage services, initially relied heavily on Amazon Web Services (AWS) to manage its massive data storage needs, handling over 500 petabytes of data (1 Petabyte = 1024 Terabytes). As the company grew, reaching 500 million users by 2016, the need for a tailored, cost-effective solution became increasingly apparent.\nProblem: While AWS provided exceptional scalability and reliability, Dropbox faced increasing costs and a desire for greater control over its infrastructure to optimize performance and enhance security.\nResolution: In 2016, Dropbox undertook a bold move by transitioning 90% of its operations from AWS to its custom-built infrastructure known as \"Magic Pocket.\" This transition involved extensive planning and negotiation with various hardware vendors to procure the necessary equipment at competitive rates.\nWhat changed in terms of cloud services?\nWhat changed in terms of cost recognition? \nWhat is the generic name of this move?\n\nDropbox shifted from the public to a private cloud\nThey replaced most of their OPEX with CAPEX\nGenerally, such move is called the “cloud repatriation”. FYI read about Cloud First vs. Cloud Smart strategies: \nhttps://www.bacancytechnology.com/blog/cloud-first-vs-cloud-smart\nNegotiation Strategy:\nVolume Discounts: Dropbox leveraged its scale to negotiate significant discounts on storage hardware, reducing costs substantially. They negotiated deals for over 1,000 petabytes of storage capacity.\nLong-term Partnerships: Unlike transactional relationships, Dropbox sought long-term partnerships with its vendors, ensuring mutual benefits and continuous support. As per the Kraljic matrix, storage would fall under the Leverage quadrant.\nPerformance Clauses: Agreements included specific performance metrics with clauses for upgrade paths and future technology integrations.\nOutcomes:\nCost Reduction: The move saved Dropbox approximately $75 million in operating costs over two years following the transition.\nPerformance Enhancement: The custom infrastructure allowed Dropbox to optimize data management and speed up file access times, reducing latency by 40% and increasing upload speeds by up to 50%.\nIncreased Control and Security: Operating its cloud infrastructure gave Dropbox increased control over its data and enhanced security measures, meeting compliance with more stringent data protection standards.","source":{"caseDeck":"Training Cases (June 2026).pptx","caseSlide":4,"courseDeck":"CIPS Technology IT Procurement Foundations Slides July 2026 (With Menti and Additional Slides).pptx","courseSlide":60}},"knowledge":{"slide-047":{"slideNumber":47,"content":"Cloud Computing"},"slide-048":{"slideNumber":48,"content":"NIST Cloud Computing Model\n\n“Cloud computing is a model for enabling ubiquitous, convenient, on-demand network access to a shared pool of configurable computing resources (e.g. networks, servers, storage, applications, and services) that can be rapidly provisioned and released with minimal management effort or service provider interaction.”"},"slide-055":{"slideNumber":55,"content":"A metric that quantifies the total workload or capacity that an individual service representative or a team can handle within a given period.\n\nFull-Service Equivalent (FSE)\n\n* When in doubt, **ask questions to clarify** – don’t guess.\n* **Strict zero-hallucination policy**: If an answer can't be sourced, say so clearly.\n* Don’t paraphrase external sources unless you have access to them directly.\n* **Use only verified sources**."},"slide-056":{"slideNumber":56,"content":"Session 3 Cloud Computing"},"slide-057":{"slideNumber":57,"content":"Session 3 Cloud Computing"},"slide-059":{"slideNumber":59,"content":"IaaS=1, PaaS =2, SaaS=3. Please select."},"slide-060":{"slideNumber":60,"content":"Dropbox's Strategic Shift from AWS (2016)\nBackground: Dropbox, a leader in cloud storage services, initially relied heavily on Amazon Web Services (AWS) to manage its massive data storage needs, handling over 500 petabytes of data (1 Petabyte = 1024 Terabytes). As the company grew, reaching 500 million users by 2016, the need for a tailored, cost-effective solution became increasingly apparent.\nProblem: While AWS provided exceptional scalability and reliability, Dropbox faced increasing costs and a desire for greater control over its infrastructure to optimize performance and enhance security.\nResolution: In 2016, Dropbox undertook a bold move by transitioning 90% of its operations from AWS to its custom-built infrastructure known as \"Magic Pocket.\" This transition involved extensive planning and negotiation with various hardware vendors to procure the necessary equipment at competitive rates.\nWhat changed in terms of cloud services?\nWhat changed in terms of cost recognition? \nWhat is the generic name of this move?\n\nDropbox shifted from the public to a private cloud\nThey replaced most of their OPEX with CAPEX\nGenerally, such move is called the “cloud repatriation”. FYI read about Cloud First vs. Cloud Smart strategies: \nhttps://www.bacancytechnology.com/blog/cloud-first-vs-cloud-smart\nNegotiation Strategy:\nVolume Discounts: Dropbox leveraged its scale to negotiate significant discounts on storage hardware, reducing costs substantially. They negotiated deals for over 1,000 petabytes of storage capacity.\nLong-term Partnerships: Unlike transactional relationships, Dropbox sought long-term partnerships with its vendors, ensuring mutual benefits and continuous support. As per the Kraljic matrix, storage would fall under the Leverage quadrant.\nPerformance Clauses: Agreements included specific performance metrics with clauses for upgrade paths and future technology integrations.\nOutcomes:\nCost Reduction: The move saved Dropbox approximately $75 million in operating costs over two years following the transition.\nPerformance Enhancement: The custom infrastructure allowed Dropbox to optimize data management and speed up file access times, reducing latency by 40% and increasing upload speeds by up to 50%.\nIncreased Control and Security: Operating its cloud infrastructure gave Dropbox increased control over its data and enhanced security measures, meeting compliance with more stringent data protection standards."},"slide-079":{"slideNumber":79,"content":"Maintenance\n\n1\n\n2\n\n3\n\n4\n\nCorrective maintenance  Rectify an actual fault\n\nPerfective maintenance Improve the value\n\nAdaptive maintenance Maintain compliance\n\nPreventative maintenance Prevent a future fault\n\nSupport"}}},"CASE-04":{"case":{"caseId":"CASE-04","caseNumber":4,"title":"Contact Center Renewal Negotiations","authoritativeCaseContent":"Case #4: Contact Center Renewal Negotiations\n\nBackground:\nState-owned company. \nBalancing strict Business SLA with cost-efficiency goals.\nThe end-user (Customer Service) established tight bonds with the supplier and implemented a few informal agreements (deviating from the contract).\nTiered pricing is based on resources/rates. Present consumption is at Tier 4.\nSLA compliance: 80% response rate mandated by the law.\nSupplier sought to recover account acquisition investments through extra charges.\n\nTier 4","studentQuestions":["What negotiation strategy would you recommend?","Should procurement challenge the SLA and staffing assumptions?","How should informal agreements be handled?"],"relatedTopics":["Sourcing Value Levers","Information Asymmetry","Types of Negotiation","Formal and Relational Contracts","IT Procurement Contracts"],"embeddedCourseGuidance":"Case #4: Contact Center Renewal Negotiations\n\nBackground:\nState-owned company. \nBalancing strict Business SLA with cost-efficiency goals.\nThe end-user (Customer Service) established tight bonds with the supplier and implemented a few informal agreements (deviating from the contract).\nTiered pricing is based on resources/rates. Present consumption is at Tier 4.\nSLA compliance: 80% response rate mandated by the law.\nSupplier sought to recover account acquisition investments through extra charges.\n\nTier 4\n\nWhat will be your negotiation strategy?\n\nSLA\nContract compliance (Tier allocation)\nResource allocation\nRates.","source":{"caseDeck":"Training Cases (June 2026).pptx","caseSlide":5,"courseDeck":"CIPS Technology IT Procurement Foundations Slides July 2026 (With Menti and Additional Slides).pptx","courseSlide":89}},"knowledge":{"slide-083":{"slideNumber":83,"content":"Sourcing of IT \u000bServices"},"slide-084":{"slideNumber":84,"content":"Session 4 Sourcing of IT Services\n\nManaged Services\n\nMobilization\n\nOccasional covering of resource gaps\n\nOutsourcing of complete teams to deliver a project or a simple business process\n\nInfrastructure-centric outsourcing of highly complex business processes or critical services\n\nQuick\n\nSlow\n\nSlowest\n\nContract\n\nHighest\n\nNormal\n\nLow\n\nHighest\n\nNormal\n\nHigh\n\nIT Outsourcing\n\nStaff Augmentation\n\nAs-a-Service\n\nControl\n\nRate card / T&M\n\nProject fee or retainer\n\nSLA-based long-term contract with incentives, possibly with the transfer of skills\n\nApplication-centric automated standard tasks in the cloud\n\nImmediate\n\nLowest\n\nLowest\n\nBoiler-plate subscription agreement\n\nCost\n\nUse case\n\nSource of the competitive advantage\nConfidential information and customer data\nCorporate strategy and decision-making.\nRisk management and compliance \nContract negotiation and key legal reviews\n\nCustomer & stakeholder relationships\nHigh-value customer management \nR&D and product design\nCritical data analytics\nPerformance governance (incl. outsourcing management)\n\nWhat critical activities would you NOT outsource?"},"slide-085":{"slideNumber":85,"content":"Session 4 Sourcing of IT Services\n\nService category\n\nInsourced\n\nManaged Services\n\nStaff Augmentation\n\nSelective Sourcing\n\naaS\n\nTypical observations\n\nIT Strategy, Governance & \nStakeholder Management\n\nMission-critical roles essential to organizations ability to delivery quality IT service will always be insourced\n\nIT Support & End-User \u000bEnablement\n\nCommodity services available in market at greater maturity than IT requirements are typically outsourced\n\nEnterprise & Solution \nArchitecture\n\nEnterprise Architecture is retained as a mission-critical strategic function for the IT organization\n\nApplication Management\nEnterprise Applications\n\nValue-add corporate platforms and apps are insource, automation & cloud adoption used for outsource\n\nApplication Management\nBusiness Applications\n\nRetain critical business skills and minimal team for agility in development, outsource day-to-day management\n\nInfrastructure Operations\n\nCommodity services available in market at greater maturity than IT requirements are typically outsourced\n\nProject Execution\n\nRetain PMO & PM team for business agility and expertise, leverage partners for on-demand capacity\n\nInnovation Management\n\nRetain core capability and leverage ecosystem for idea generation and agile execution"},"slide-086":{"slideNumber":86,"content":"Outsourcing strategy\n\nFormal\n\nRelational\n\nStaff \naugmentation\n\nBPO\n\nManaged service\n\nOffshore\n\nNearshore\n\nOnshore/\nonsite\n\nInfrastructure\n\nApplication\n\nEnd-user support\n\nSecurity\n\nPartnership\n\nMarket\n\nHierarchy\n\nT&M\n\nCost Plus\n\nTarget price\n\nFirm fixed price\n\nLong\n\nMedium\n\nShort\n\nCore capabilities\n\nSkills\n\nInnovation\n\nCost reduction\n\nAs-a-service"},"slide-087":{"slideNumber":87,"content":"Session 4 Sourcing of IT Services\n\nMaintenance (L2)\n\nONSITE / LANDED\n\nONSHORE/\nNEARHSORE \n(NEIGHBORING COUNTRY)\n\nProject scope\n\nAnalysis & Architecture\n\nAcceptance testing\n\nImplementation\n\nHigh-level design\n\nDevelopment/ \nunit testing\n\nSystem testing\n\nOFFSHORE \n(DISTANT COUNTRY)\n\nLOW-COST\n\nGlobal support (L3)\n\nSupport (L1)\n\nHIGH-COST"},"slide-088":{"slideNumber":88,"content":"Session 4 Sourcing of IT Services\n\nProgram / Project portfolio\n\nProject 1\n\nProject 2\n\nProject 3\n\nProject 4\n\nProject 5\n\nQuality Assurance\n\nSolution Architecture\n\nProgram Management Office, Governance (Reviews, Reports, Dashboards)\n\nSingle Billing & Invoicing\n\nProject scrum management\n\nDomain experts/ \nBusiness analyst\n\nProject delivery teams\n\nHorizontal services"},"slide-089":{"slideNumber":89,"content":"Case #4: Contact Center Renewal Negotiations\n\nBackground:\nState-owned company. \nBalancing strict Business SLA with cost-efficiency goals.\nThe end-user (Customer Service) established tight bonds with the supplier and implemented a few informal agreements (deviating from the contract).\nTiered pricing is based on resources/rates. Present consumption is at Tier 4.\nSLA compliance: 80% response rate mandated by the law.\nSupplier sought to recover account acquisition investments through extra charges.\n\nTier 4\n\nWhat will be your negotiation strategy?\n\nSLA\nContract compliance (Tier allocation)\nResource allocation\nRates."},"slide-096":{"slideNumber":96,"content":""},"slide-097":{"slideNumber":97,"content":"Session 5 Source-to-Contract\n\nComply \u000bLevers\n\nDemand\u000bLevers\n\nSource\u000bLevers\n\nFulfil\u000bLevers\n\nManage\u000bLevers\n\nProcess\u000bLevers\n\nSpend \ngovernance\n\nEliminate \ndemand\n\nType of sourcing event\n\nBundle/unbundle\n\nDemand / supply balancing\n\nP2P control\n\nPolicy compliance\n\nReview quantity\n\nCycle time\n\nSupplier rationalization\n\nOrdering\n\nVariations\n\nReduce frequency\n\nPrioritize projects\n\nVolume or time commitment\n\nPhysical delivery\n\nRisk\n\nStandardize specs\n\nBuying channels\n\nPricing mechanism\n\nStorage and distribution\n\nPerformance\n\nReduce portfolio\n\nSource from OEM\n\nPerformance or benefit incentive\n\nContract compliance\n\nValue delivery\n\nConsider alternatives\n\nRisk allocation\n\nRepair and replacement\n\nAdditional benefits (revenue, marketing, etc)\n\nEncourage reuse\n\nPayment terms\n\nTransaction cost\n\nInitiate a sourcing project\n\nIdentify business needs and study \u000bthe market\n\nSpecify requirements\n\nDefine a sourcing strategy\n\nSelect a supplier and award a contract\n\nManage a contract and supplier relationship\n\nSourcing \nvalue levers"},"slide-100":{"slideNumber":100,"content":"Software price\n\nProfitability\n\nOwnership\n\nCref\t\nCnew =                       x TAnew\n                    TAref"},"slide-104":{"slideNumber":104,"content":"Scenario Overview:\nA buyer invests in specialized equipment to increase profits (P). The supplier may raise prices (R), leading to different outcomes depending on whether the buyer continues with the supplier or switches.\nKey Variables:\n• P = increased profits\n• V = investment cost\n• R = price increase by supplier\n• B = loss of business for supplier\nScenario 1: Buyer Invests; Supplier Does Not Raise Prices\n• Buyer Payoff: P - V\n• Supplier Payoff: £0\nOutcome: Positive for Buyer if P > V; Neutral for Supplier\nScenario 2: Supplier Raises Prices\n• Buyer Payoff: P – V - R\n• Supplier Payoff: R\nOutcome: Positive if P-V-R > £0; Negative otherwise\nScenario 3: Buyer Switches Suppliers After Price Increase\n• Buyer Payoff: P-V-R-(P-R)=  -V \n• Supplier Payoff: R – B \nOutcome: Negative for Both Buyer and Supplier. DON'T INVEST.\n\n• In Scenario 3, both parties suffer losses, showing the importance of contract design and trust.\n\nWhat are the possible solutions to overcome the hold-up problem?\n\nVertical integration (insourcing)\nLong-term contracts fixing critical (red-flag) terms\nRevenue share"},"slide-105":{"slideNumber":105,"content":"Session 5 Source-to-Contract\n\nTransaction spectrum\n\nCompany size\n\nRelational contracts\n\nFormal contracts\n\nSmall\n\nCorporate\n\nRelational\n\nDiscrete\n\nFirm fixed price\n\nCost plus\n\nTarget price\n\nTime and material\n\n“ … contractual relations, not discrete contract, always have and will always be the dominant form of exchange behavior in the society. ” (Macneil 1985)"},"slide-106":{"slideNumber":106,"content":"Session 5 Source-to-Contract"}}},"CASE-05":{"case":{"caseId":"CASE-05","caseNumber":5,"title":"SAP, Diageo, Mars & Oracle License Disputes","authoritativeCaseContent":"Diageo has licensed mySAP Enterprise Resource Planning (ERP) software from SAP since 2004, using its functionality to manage the manufacturing, stock and supply chain, financial reporting, and human resources requirements of its business. \nThrough November 2015, Diageo paid SAP between £ 50 million and £ 61 million in license and maintenance fees. Its business's manufacturing, stock and supply chain, financial reporting, and human resources requirements. Pursuant to the Software License and Maintenance Agreement between Diageo and SAP (the Agreement), the fees were priced according to the number of \"Named Users\" of the software.  Named Users were defined in the Agreement as individuals who are \"authorized to access the Software directly or indirectly,\" depending on their user category as set out in a schedule to the Agreement.  The Agreement also granted Diageo a license to use SAP Exchange Infrastructure (SAP PI), which distributed messages between ERP and other SAP systems.  Diageo paid an additional fee to use SAP PI based on the monthly volume of messages processed.\nFrom around 2011, Diageo developed and introduced two new software systems, \"Connect\" and \"Gen2\", using a platform provided by Salesforce. Connect enabled Diageo's customers and distributors to place orders for products directly using an online portal rather than through Diageo employees in a call center. Gen2 was used to manage the operations of Diageo's sales and services representatives.\nDiageo accepted that Gen2 and Connect interacted with the ERP software via the SAP PI system but disputed whether that interaction constituted use and/or direct or indirect access to the ERP software to give rise to the payment of additional fees. SAP claimed that Gen2 and Connect used and/or accessed the ERP software directly or indirectly without SAP being appropriately compensated under the Named User pricing arrangement. As a result, SAP claimed additional license and maintenance fees of £54,503,578. The court sided with SAP on liability but did not make a ruling on the amount of compensation due to SAP.\n\nCase #5: SAP and Diageo's License Dispute (2017)\n\nMars vs. Oracle License Dispute (2014-2015)\n\nSeptember 2014:\nOracle's License Management Services (LMS) initiated a \"License Review\" of Mars Inc.’s usage of Oracle software on VMware environments.\nMay 2015:\nOracle issued successive termination notices to Mars, beginning the formal audit process and highlighting potential breaches of licensing agreements due to the use of VMware’s VMotion technology​.\nOctober 23, 2015:\nMars filed a lawsuit in the San Francisco Superior Court seeking a judicial declaration that it did not breach its agreement and requesting a preliminary injunction to prevent Oracle from terminating its licenses.\nNovember 2015:\nMars and Oracle reached a stipulation where Oracle agreed not to terminate Mars’ licenses. Subsequently, Mars requested the dismissal of its case with prejudice, effectively ending the legal proceedings without a court ruling on the merits.","studentQuestions":["What procurement mistakes contributed to these disputes?","What is indirect access risk?","How should buyers protect themselves contractually?"],"relatedTopics":["ISO 19770-1 ITAM","Information Asymmetry","Incomplete Contracts","IT Procurement Contracts","SaaS License Metrics"],"embeddedCourseGuidance":"Diageo has licensed mySAP Enterprise Resource Planning (ERP) software from SAP since 2004, using its functionality to manage the manufacturing, stock and supply chain, financial reporting, and human resources requirements of its business. \nThrough November 2015, Diageo paid SAP between £ 50 million and £ 61 million in license and maintenance fees. Its business's manufacturing, stock and supply chain, financial reporting, and human resources requirements. Pursuant to the Software License and Maintenance Agreement between Diageo and SAP (the Agreement), the fees were priced according to the number of \"Named Users\" of the software.  Named Users were defined in the Agreement as individuals who are \"authorized to access the Software directly or indirectly,\" depending on their user category as set out in a schedule to the Agreement.  The Agreement also granted Diageo a license to use SAP Exchange Infrastructure (SAP PI), which distributed messages between ERP and other SAP systems.  Diageo paid an additional fee to use SAP PI based on the monthly volume of messages processed.\nFrom around 2011, Diageo developed and introduced two new software systems, \"Connect\" and \"Gen2\", using a platform provided by Salesforce. Connect enabled Diageo's customers and distributors to place orders for products directly using an online portal rather than through Diageo employees in a call center. Gen2 was used to manage the operations of Diageo's sales and services representatives.\nDiageo accepted that Gen2 and Connect interacted with the ERP software via the SAP PI system but disputed whether that interaction constituted use and/or direct or indirect access to the ERP software to give rise to the payment of additional fees. SAP claimed that Gen2 and Connect used and/or accessed the ERP software directly or indirectly without SAP being appropriately compensated under the Named User pricing arrangement. As a result, SAP claimed additional license and maintenance fees of £54,503,578. The court sided with SAP on liability but did not make a ruling on the amount of compensation due to SAP.\n\nCase #5: SAP and Diageo's License Dispute (2017)\n\nMars vs. Oracle License Dispute (2014-2015)\n\nSeptember 2014:\nOracle's License Management Services (LMS) initiated a \"License Review\" of Mars Inc.’s usage of Oracle software on VMware environments.\nMay 2015:\nOracle issued successive termination notices to Mars, beginning the formal audit process and highlighting potential breaches of licensing agreements due to the use of VMware’s VMotion technology​.\nOctober 23, 2015:\nMars filed a lawsuit in the San Francisco Superior Court seeking a judicial declaration that it did not breach its agreement and requesting a preliminary injunction to prevent Oracle from terminating its licenses.\nNovember 2015:\nMars and Oracle reached a stipulation where Oracle agreed not to terminate Mars’ licenses. Subsequently, Mars requested the dismissal of its case with prejudice, effectively ending the legal proceedings without a court ruling on the merits.","source":{"caseDeck":"Training Cases (June 2026).pptx","caseSlide":6,"courseDeck":"CIPS Technology IT Procurement Foundations Slides July 2026 (With Menti and Additional Slides).pptx","courseSlide":94}},"knowledge":{"slide-062":{"slideNumber":62,"content":"Session 3 Cloud Computing\n\nRun\n\nSprint 5\n\nSprint 4\n\nSprint 3\n\nAGILE\n\nWATERFALL\n\nValue \u000bCreated\n\nSprint 2\n\nSprint 1\n\nD\n\nP\n\nAgile is both incremental and iterative:\nIncremental: The product is built piece by piece (increments). Each sprint delivers a usable, working part of the final solution, adding functionality over time.\nIterative: Each increment can be refined and improved through feedback and repeated cycles (iterations). Teams don’t try to design the “perfect” solution upfront; they learn and adjust with every sprint.\nAgile combines the two:\u000b👉 Iteration = refining and evolving through cycles.\u000b👉 Increment = delivering value in usable chunks."},"slide-063":{"slideNumber":63,"content":"The number of users/clients/instances, where “client” means device and “instance” means an installation or use of the software\nUser role (guest, general user, power user, admin) \nNamed User\nClient Access License (CAL)\n\nUser\n\nType of machine (desktop, thin client, physical or virtual server)\nSize of a machine (cores, processors, sockets)\nHost ID\nToken\n\nCapacity / Hardware\n\nPay-as-you-go (per use, per transaction or else)\nHard / soft capped (e.g., up to X transactions during a license period and then a hard stop or paying an overage rate)\nCompany metrics\nEmployees / transactions / locations\nRevenue (self-declared or from an annual report)\n\nConsumption\n\nUnlimited everything (ULA – Unlimited License Agreement) \nUnlimited users / capacity / consumption, where unlimited users capped on transactions, and so on\n\nUnlimited\n\nCloud credits\n\nCloud\n\nFeature\n\nFeature-based \nFloating feature\n\nAI Features\n\nFlat fee\nAnnual commitment\nInput/output tokens\nAPI calls"},"slide-092":{"slideNumber":92,"content":"Source-to-Contract"},"slide-093":{"slideNumber":93,"content":"Session 5 Source-to-Contract\n\nService Level and Risk Management\n\nContract management\n\nFinancial management\n\nRelease and deploy\n\nRetire\n\nMaintain\n\nAcquire or develop\n\nSpecify\n\nChange management\n\nSecurity management\n\nData management\n\nLicense management\n\nOrganization\n\nLeadership\n\nSupport\n\nOperation\n\nPlanning\n\nPerformance\n\nImprovement\n\n2 – LIFECYCLE INTEGRATION\n\n1 – THRUSTWORTHY DATA\n\nPlan\n\nDo\n\nCheck\n\nAct\n\n3 - OPTIMIZATION\n\nISO 19770-1:2017\nMANAGEMENT PROCESSES\n\nCOST SAVINGS\n\nCONTROL\n\nVendor management"},"slide-097":{"slideNumber":97,"content":"Session 5 Source-to-Contract\n\nComply \u000bLevers\n\nDemand\u000bLevers\n\nSource\u000bLevers\n\nFulfil\u000bLevers\n\nManage\u000bLevers\n\nProcess\u000bLevers\n\nSpend \ngovernance\n\nEliminate \ndemand\n\nType of sourcing event\n\nBundle/unbundle\n\nDemand / supply balancing\n\nP2P control\n\nPolicy compliance\n\nReview quantity\n\nCycle time\n\nSupplier rationalization\n\nOrdering\n\nVariations\n\nReduce frequency\n\nPrioritize projects\n\nVolume or time commitment\n\nPhysical delivery\n\nRisk\n\nStandardize specs\n\nBuying channels\n\nPricing mechanism\n\nStorage and distribution\n\nPerformance\n\nReduce portfolio\n\nSource from OEM\n\nPerformance or benefit incentive\n\nContract compliance\n\nValue delivery\n\nConsider alternatives\n\nRisk allocation\n\nRepair and replacement\n\nAdditional benefits (revenue, marketing, etc)\n\nEncourage reuse\n\nPayment terms\n\nTransaction cost\n\nInitiate a sourcing project\n\nIdentify business needs and study \u000bthe market\n\nSpecify requirements\n\nDefine a sourcing strategy\n\nSelect a supplier and award a contract\n\nManage a contract and supplier relationship\n\nSourcing \nvalue levers"},"slide-101":{"slideNumber":101,"content":"Ub\n\nUa\n\nUb\n\nUa\n\nDistributive negotiations \n(fixed pie, win-lose, zero-sum games)\n\nIntegrative negotiations \n(win-win, cooperative games)\n\nU1\n\nU2\n\nU3\n\nU4\n\nimprovement\n\nconcession\n\nClaiming value\n\nCreating value\n\nTypes of Negotiation\n\nSession 5 Source-to-Contract\n\nKey Tactics in Cooperative Games:\nInterest Exploration: Understanding each party's underlying interests rather than focusing on positions.\nSharing Information: Revealing relevant information to find common ground and identify creative solutions.\nBuilding Trust: Encouraging transparency and openness to facilitate more effective problem-solving.\nJoint Problem-Solving: Discussing solutions benefiting both parties, like finding ways to cut costs without compromising quality.\nCreating Multiple Options: Propose a variety of possible outcomes and then evaluate them together to choose the best one.\n\nKey Tactics in Zero-Sum Games:\nAggressive Anchoring: Start with an extreme initial offer to influence the negotiation range.\nClaiming Value: Focus on maximizing your share of the fixed pie rather than expanding it.\nWithholding Information: Only reveal information that strengthens your position.\nUsing Leverage: Emphasize your power, options, and alternatives to push for a better outcome.\n\nWhat quadrants of the Kraljic matrix does each type of negotiation fit?"},"slide-102":{"slideNumber":102,"content":"Incomplete contracts\n\nDiscrete and relational contracting"},"slide-106":{"slideNumber":106,"content":"Session 5 Source-to-Contract"}}},"CASE-06":{"case":{"caseId":"CASE-06","caseNumber":6,"title":"IBM vs State of Indiana Welfare Transformation","authoritativeCaseContent":"Case #6: The dispute between IBM and the State of Indiana (2006-2012)\nHistory:\nContract Signing (2006): The State of Indiana signed a 10-year, $1.37 billion contract with IBM to modernize its welfare system. The project aimed to centralize and automate processing applications for food stamps, Medicaid, and other benefits.\nImplementation Challenges: Issues started to emerge almost immediately after the project began. IBM faced challenges meeting deadlines, delivering on promised functionalities, and staying within budget. The system experienced technical glitches, delays, and performance issues.\nEarly Termination (2009): In 2009, then-Governor Mitch Daniels announced the termination of the contract with IBM, citing concerns about the system's performance and IBM's failure to meet contractual obligations. The decision to terminate the contract three years into the 10-year term was unprecedented and led to a protracted legal battle.\nLegal Dispute:\nIBM's Claims:\nIBM claimed that it had met its contractual obligations and blamed Indiana for the project's failure. IBM argued that the state's changing requirements and lack of cooperation contributed to the challenges faced during implementation.\nIBM sought $53 million in unpaid fees and damages, asserting that it had invested significant resources in the project and was entitled to compensation. \nThe outcome of IBM's claim was not publicly disclosed. However, IBM likely received some compensation as part of the overall settlement agreement between the parties.\nIndiana's Claims:\nIndiana alleged that IBM had failed to deliver on its promises and had misrepresented its capabilities during the procurement process. The state argued that IBM's poor performance had cost taxpayers millions of dollars.\nIndiana sought $170 million in damages from IBM, claiming that the company's actions had caused significant harm to the state's welfare system and its beneficiaries.\nIn October 2012, an arbitration panel ruled in favor of Indiana, awarding the state $52 million in damages. This amount was significantly less than Indiana had initially sought but still represented a significant victory for the state.\nHowever, IBM challenged the arbitration award in court, leading to further legal proceedings and appeals. The exact outcome of Indiana's claims, including the total amount recovered by the state, was not publicly disclosed.","studentQuestions":["What lessons can be learned?","What governance failures contributed to the outcome?","How could the project have been structured differently?"],"relatedTopics":["Digitalization Paradox","ITIL Service Lifecycle","Integrated Procurement Planning","Incomplete Contracts","Relational Contracts"],"embeddedCourseGuidance":"Case #6: The dispute between IBM and the State of Indiana (2006-2012)\nHistory:\nContract Signing (2006): The State of Indiana signed a 10-year, $1.37 billion contract with IBM to modernize its welfare system. The project aimed to centralize and automate processing applications for food stamps, Medicaid, and other benefits.\nImplementation Challenges: Issues started to emerge almost immediately after the project began. IBM faced challenges meeting deadlines, delivering on promised functionalities, and staying within budget. The system experienced technical glitches, delays, and performance issues.\nEarly Termination (2009): In 2009, then-Governor Mitch Daniels announced the termination of the contract with IBM, citing concerns about the system's performance and IBM's failure to meet contractual obligations. The decision to terminate the contract three years into the 10-year term was unprecedented and led to a protracted legal battle.\nLegal Dispute:\nIBM's Claims:\nIBM claimed that it had met its contractual obligations and blamed Indiana for the project's failure. IBM argued that the state's changing requirements and lack of cooperation contributed to the challenges faced during implementation.\nIBM sought $53 million in unpaid fees and damages, asserting that it had invested significant resources in the project and was entitled to compensation. \nThe outcome of IBM's claim was not publicly disclosed. However, IBM likely received some compensation as part of the overall settlement agreement between the parties.\nIndiana's Claims:\nIndiana alleged that IBM had failed to deliver on its promises and had misrepresented its capabilities during the procurement process. The state argued that IBM's poor performance had cost taxpayers millions of dollars.\nIndiana sought $170 million in damages from IBM, claiming that the company's actions had caused significant harm to the state's welfare system and its beneficiaries.\nIn October 2012, an arbitration panel ruled in favor of Indiana, awarding the state $52 million in damages. This amount was significantly less than Indiana had initially sought but still represented a significant victory for the state.\nHowever, IBM challenged the arbitration award in court, leading to further legal proceedings and appeals. The exact outcome of Indiana's claims, including the total amount recovered by the state, was not publicly disclosed.\nPlease suggest lessons learned.\n\nPoor supplier selection (Indiana “had misrepresented IBM’s capabilities during the procurement process,” too many failed promises.\nIncomplete contract (“IBM claimed that it had met its contractual obligations,” Indiana claimed, “IBM's failure to meet contractual obligations.” \nChanging requirements (scope freeze not happening.)\nFailed relational governance (IBM “failed to deliver on its promises,” Indiana’s “lack of cooperation,” “promised functionality.”)\nDigitalization paradox (IBM faced challenges staying within the budget and meeting deadlines.)\nDestroyed relationships – IBM claimed $53 million based on a $1.37 billion contract.","source":{"caseDeck":"Training Cases (June 2026).pptx","caseSlide":7,"courseDeck":"CIPS Technology IT Procurement Foundations Slides July 2026 (With Menti and Additional Slides).pptx","courseSlide":109}},"knowledge":{"slide-036":{"slideNumber":36,"content":"Integrated \u000bProcurement Planning"},"slide-037":{"slideNumber":37,"content":"Source: ITIL® is a (registered) Trademark of AXELOS Limited.\n\nService strategy\n\nService Knowledge Management System (SKMS) \nincluding the Service Portfolio and Service Catalogue\n\nService design\n\nService transition\n\nService operation\n\nREQUIREMENTS\n\nTHE BUSINESS / CUSTOMERS\n\nOperational plans\n\nImprovement actions & plans\n\nTransition plans\n\nSKMS updated\n\nTested solutions\n\nSolution designs\n\nArchitectures\n\nStandards\n\nSDPs\n\nOperational services\n\nStrategies\n\nPolicies\n\nResource and constraints\n\nSLPs from Requirements\n\nContinual service improvement"},"slide-038":{"slideNumber":38,"content":""},"slide-040":{"slideNumber":40,"content":"When does a project end?"},"slide-041":{"slideNumber":41,"content":"Session 2 Integrated Procurement Planning"},"slide-043":{"slideNumber":43,"content":"The fundamental economic problem is scarcity. Wants are unlimited, and resources are finite, so choices must be made. Resources must be used and distributed optimally. This gives rise to opportunity cost. The opportunity cost of a choice is the value of the next best alternative forgone. \nIn 2016, British Airways outsourced its UK-based IT staff (over 700 people) as part of its business strategy. \nThis outsourcing strategy allegedly resulted in a 2017 global flight disruption that affected 75,000 passengers and caused a nearly £150 million loss.\nPreviously, BA claimed a 25% reduction in its IT operational costs. However, due to some simple considerations, it’s unclear if the outsourcing risk exposure has been accounted for in the business case. \nA typical large company's IT budget (over $1 billion in revenues) is 2% of revenues, translating into approximately £210 million for BA in 2016. Let's assume 50% of that is related to infrastructure operations, maintenance, support, security, etc. A 25% saving corresponds to just £27.5 million vs. the 2017 crisis cost of £150 million.\nLet's assume a business case as follows.\n\nOpportunity cost & TCO (British Airways IT Outsourcing)\n\nWhat would you recommend - outsourcing or insourcing?\nWhat could be the opportunity cost?\n\n2017 risk exposure of £150 million makes this business case break even.  \nThe average revenue per employee in the airline industry in 2016 was £350,000 * 700 * 5 years = £1,225 billion.\n\nWith a profit margin of 5.1% in the airline industry in 2016, £1 savings = £ 19.61 in revenue. The opportunity cost is £1,225 billion / £19.61 = £62.5 million."},"slide-083":{"slideNumber":83,"content":"Sourcing of IT \u000bServices"},"slide-084":{"slideNumber":84,"content":"Session 4 Sourcing of IT Services\n\nManaged Services\n\nMobilization\n\nOccasional covering of resource gaps\n\nOutsourcing of complete teams to deliver a project or a simple business process\n\nInfrastructure-centric outsourcing of highly complex business processes or critical services\n\nQuick\n\nSlow\n\nSlowest\n\nContract\n\nHighest\n\nNormal\n\nLow\n\nHighest\n\nNormal\n\nHigh\n\nIT Outsourcing\n\nStaff Augmentation\n\nAs-a-Service\n\nControl\n\nRate card / T&M\n\nProject fee or retainer\n\nSLA-based long-term contract with incentives, possibly with the transfer of skills\n\nApplication-centric automated standard tasks in the cloud\n\nImmediate\n\nLowest\n\nLowest\n\nBoiler-plate subscription agreement\n\nCost\n\nUse case\n\nSource of the competitive advantage\nConfidential information and customer data\nCorporate strategy and decision-making.\nRisk management and compliance \nContract negotiation and key legal reviews\n\nCustomer & stakeholder relationships\nHigh-value customer management \nR&D and product design\nCritical data analytics\nPerformance governance (incl. outsourcing management)\n\nWhat critical activities would you NOT outsource?"},"slide-085":{"slideNumber":85,"content":"Session 4 Sourcing of IT Services\n\nService category\n\nInsourced\n\nManaged Services\n\nStaff Augmentation\n\nSelective Sourcing\n\naaS\n\nTypical observations\n\nIT Strategy, Governance & \nStakeholder Management\n\nMission-critical roles essential to organizations ability to delivery quality IT service will always be insourced\n\nIT Support & End-User \u000bEnablement\n\nCommodity services available in market at greater maturity than IT requirements are typically outsourced\n\nEnterprise & Solution \nArchitecture\n\nEnterprise Architecture is retained as a mission-critical strategic function for the IT organization\n\nApplication Management\nEnterprise Applications\n\nValue-add corporate platforms and apps are insource, automation & cloud adoption used for outsource\n\nApplication Management\nBusiness Applications\n\nRetain critical business skills and minimal team for agility in development, outsource day-to-day management\n\nInfrastructure Operations\n\nCommodity services available in market at greater maturity than IT requirements are typically outsourced\n\nProject Execution\n\nRetain PMO & PM team for business agility and expertise, leverage partners for on-demand capacity\n\nInnovation Management\n\nRetain core capability and leverage ecosystem for idea generation and agile execution"},"slide-097":{"slideNumber":97,"content":"Session 5 Source-to-Contract\n\nComply \u000bLevers\n\nDemand\u000bLevers\n\nSource\u000bLevers\n\nFulfil\u000bLevers\n\nManage\u000bLevers\n\nProcess\u000bLevers\n\nSpend \ngovernance\n\nEliminate \ndemand\n\nType of sourcing event\n\nBundle/unbundle\n\nDemand / supply balancing\n\nP2P control\n\nPolicy compliance\n\nReview quantity\n\nCycle time\n\nSupplier rationalization\n\nOrdering\n\nVariations\n\nReduce frequency\n\nPrioritize projects\n\nVolume or time commitment\n\nPhysical delivery\n\nRisk\n\nStandardize specs\n\nBuying channels\n\nPricing mechanism\n\nStorage and distribution\n\nPerformance\n\nReduce portfolio\n\nSource from OEM\n\nPerformance or benefit incentive\n\nContract compliance\n\nValue delivery\n\nConsider alternatives\n\nRisk allocation\n\nRepair and replacement\n\nAdditional benefits (revenue, marketing, etc)\n\nEncourage reuse\n\nPayment terms\n\nTransaction cost\n\nInitiate a sourcing project\n\nIdentify business needs and study \u000bthe market\n\nSpecify requirements\n\nDefine a sourcing strategy\n\nSelect a supplier and award a contract\n\nManage a contract and supplier relationship\n\nSourcing \nvalue levers"},"slide-101":{"slideNumber":101,"content":"Ub\n\nUa\n\nUb\n\nUa\n\nDistributive negotiations \n(fixed pie, win-lose, zero-sum games)\n\nIntegrative negotiations \n(win-win, cooperative games)\n\nU1\n\nU2\n\nU3\n\nU4\n\nimprovement\n\nconcession\n\nClaiming value\n\nCreating value\n\nTypes of Negotiation\n\nSession 5 Source-to-Contract\n\nKey Tactics in Cooperative Games:\nInterest Exploration: Understanding each party's underlying interests rather than focusing on positions.\nSharing Information: Revealing relevant information to find common ground and identify creative solutions.\nBuilding Trust: Encouraging transparency and openness to facilitate more effective problem-solving.\nJoint Problem-Solving: Discussing solutions benefiting both parties, like finding ways to cut costs without compromising quality.\nCreating Multiple Options: Propose a variety of possible outcomes and then evaluate them together to choose the best one.\n\nKey Tactics in Zero-Sum Games:\nAggressive Anchoring: Start with an extreme initial offer to influence the negotiation range.\nClaiming Value: Focus on maximizing your share of the fixed pie rather than expanding it.\nWithholding Information: Only reveal information that strengthens your position.\nUsing Leverage: Emphasize your power, options, and alternatives to push for a better outcome.\n\nWhat quadrants of the Kraljic matrix does each type of negotiation fit?"},"slide-104":{"slideNumber":104,"content":"Scenario Overview:\nA buyer invests in specialized equipment to increase profits (P). The supplier may raise prices (R), leading to different outcomes depending on whether the buyer continues with the supplier or switches.\nKey Variables:\n• P = increased profits\n• V = investment cost\n• R = price increase by supplier\n• B = loss of business for supplier\nScenario 1: Buyer Invests; Supplier Does Not Raise Prices\n• Buyer Payoff: P - V\n• Supplier Payoff: £0\nOutcome: Positive for Buyer if P > V; Neutral for Supplier\nScenario 2: Supplier Raises Prices\n• Buyer Payoff: P – V - R\n• Supplier Payoff: R\nOutcome: Positive if P-V-R > £0; Negative otherwise\nScenario 3: Buyer Switches Suppliers After Price Increase\n• Buyer Payoff: P-V-R-(P-R)=  -V \n• Supplier Payoff: R – B \nOutcome: Negative for Both Buyer and Supplier. DON'T INVEST.\n\n• In Scenario 3, both parties suffer losses, showing the importance of contract design and trust.\n\nWhat are the possible solutions to overcome the hold-up problem?\n\nVertical integration (insourcing)\nLong-term contracts fixing critical (red-flag) terms\nRevenue share"},"slide-105":{"slideNumber":105,"content":"Session 5 Source-to-Contract\n\nTransaction spectrum\n\nCompany size\n\nRelational contracts\n\nFormal contracts\n\nSmall\n\nCorporate\n\nRelational\n\nDiscrete\n\nFirm fixed price\n\nCost plus\n\nTarget price\n\nTime and material\n\n“ … contractual relations, not discrete contract, always have and will always be the dominant form of exchange behavior in the society. ” (Macneil 1985)"},"slide-106":{"slideNumber":106,"content":"Session 5 Source-to-Contract"},"slide-108":{"slideNumber":108,"content":"Session 5 Source-to-Contract\n\n1. Licensing Scope & Definitions\nClear delineation of what’s licensed—models, APIs, datasets, inputs, outputs.\n2. Vendor Usage Rights\nLimits on vendor using your data (inputs/outputs) for training, analytics, or other customers.\n3. Confidentiality & Data Governance\nRobust privacy measures, audit rights, secure handling of proprietary or sensitive info.\n4. IP & Ownership\nClear ownership of AI-generated content; outputs may not be copyrightable.\n5. Restrictions on Competing Models\nAvoid clauses that block you from building or using competing AI tools with the outputs.\n6. IP Infringement & Indemnity\nRisk of outputs containing copyrighted content—negotiate indemnities, warranties, filters.\n7. SLAs – Uptime, Latency, Accuracy\nManageable service levels with transparent disclaimers; adjust expectations to reality.\n8. Warranties & Disclaimers\nUnderstand that vendors typically disclaim correctness; any performance warranties should be explicit.\n\n9. Change Control / Versioning\nPrevent unilateral vendor changes—require notice or customer consent for material updates.\n10. Data Portability / Exit Rights\nEnsure you can retrieve input/output data and model artefacts on termination.\n11. Security & Monitoring\nEvaluate vendor’s monitoring practices and potential conflicts with confidentiality.\n12. Audit Rights\nContractual right to audit vendor performance, including RAG data pipelines.\n13. Compliance / Legal Uses\nClarify that your use of tool is compliant (e.g., GDPR, export controls).\n14. Limitations of Liability\nAdjust caps and exceptions (e.g., for IP infringement, data breaches).\n15. Insurance & Coverage\nRequire indemnity for legal/regulatory claims; ask if vendor has relevant insurance.\n16. Dispute Resolution & Termination\nDefine exit triggers (e.g., output accuracy breaches, IP claims) and dispute mechanisms.\n\nSeparate SLA clauses can be introduced, e.g.,\nHallucination SLA: AI provider ensures ≤X% fabricated facts in verified test cases.\nAccuracy SLA: AI must achieve ≥Y% factual accuracy within defined benchmarks.\nValidation & Audit Rights: Buyer may audit sample outputs for hallucinations and inaccuracies.\nCorrective Actions: AI vendor must retrain or fine-tune models when hallucination rates exceed thresholds."},"slide-109":{"slideNumber":109,"content":"Case #6: The dispute between IBM and the State of Indiana (2006-2012)\nHistory:\nContract Signing (2006): The State of Indiana signed a 10-year, $1.37 billion contract with IBM to modernize its welfare system. The project aimed to centralize and automate processing applications for food stamps, Medicaid, and other benefits.\nImplementation Challenges: Issues started to emerge almost immediately after the project began. IBM faced challenges meeting deadlines, delivering on promised functionalities, and staying within budget. The system experienced technical glitches, delays, and performance issues.\nEarly Termination (2009): In 2009, then-Governor Mitch Daniels announced the termination of the contract with IBM, citing concerns about the system's performance and IBM's failure to meet contractual obligations. The decision to terminate the contract three years into the 10-year term was unprecedented and led to a protracted legal battle.\nLegal Dispute:\nIBM's Claims:\nIBM claimed that it had met its contractual obligations and blamed Indiana for the project's failure. IBM argued that the state's changing requirements and lack of cooperation contributed to the challenges faced during implementation.\nIBM sought $53 million in unpaid fees and damages, asserting that it had invested significant resources in the project and was entitled to compensation. \nThe outcome of IBM's claim was not publicly disclosed. However, IBM likely received some compensation as part of the overall settlement agreement between the parties.\nIndiana's Claims:\nIndiana alleged that IBM had failed to deliver on its promises and had misrepresented its capabilities during the procurement process. The state argued that IBM's poor performance had cost taxpayers millions of dollars.\nIndiana sought $170 million in damages from IBM, claiming that the company's actions had caused significant harm to the state's welfare system and its beneficiaries.\nIn October 2012, an arbitration panel ruled in favor of Indiana, awarding the state $52 million in damages. This amount was significantly less than Indiana had initially sought but still represented a significant victory for the state.\nHowever, IBM challenged the arbitration award in court, leading to further legal proceedings and appeals. The exact outcome of Indiana's claims, including the total amount recovered by the state, was not publicly disclosed.\nPlease suggest lessons learned.\n\nPoor supplier selection (Indiana “had misrepresented IBM’s capabilities during the procurement process,” too many failed promises.\nIncomplete contract (“IBM claimed that it had met its contractual obligations,” Indiana claimed, “IBM's failure to meet contractual obligations.” \nChanging requirements (scope freeze not happening.)\nFailed relational governance (IBM “failed to deliver on its promises,” Indiana’s “lack of cooperation,” “promised functionality.”)\nDigitalization paradox (IBM faced challenges staying within the budget and meeting deadlines.)\nDestroyed relationships – IBM claimed $53 million based on a $1.37 billion contract."}}},"CASE-07":{"case":{"caseId":"CASE-07","caseNumber":7,"title":"NHS National Program for IT","authoritativeCaseContent":"The national program for IT in the health service is the largest civilian computer project in the world. It was spawned in late 2001. A far-reaching vision set out a program that would supposedly transform people’s healthcare experiences. Hospital admissions and appointments would be booked online. Pharmacists would no longer struggle with GPs’ indecipherable handwriting, and drug prescriptions would be handled electronically.\nPoliticians settled on a program lasting two years and nine months, from April 2003 until December 2005. Given the extent of the proposals, that was unrealistic. \nThe market research concluded that no single player could become a prime contractor in this program. NHS soon announced that the procurement process for the program would be structured to attract global IT players. These players would not get paid until they delivered, and those not up to the mark would be replaced. In May 2003, potential bidders were given a 500-page document called a draft output-based specification and told to respond within five weeks.\nIn December 2003, the value of the contracts had already shot up to £6.2 billion from the original £2.3 billion. The time scale had tripled in length, and instead of the two years and nine months from April 2003 originally promised—the contracts were now to run for 10 years.\nFour winning bidders were appointed: BT and Fujitsu with a US software firm, IDX, and Accenture and CSC with a British software company called iSoft, whose flagship software system, Lorenzo, would be “available from early 2004.” Lorenzo caused a big headache for Accenture, which had two contracts worth around £1 billion each. Eventually, Accenture and CSC produced a confidential report in February 2006 stating that Lorenzo had “no mapping of features to release, nor detailed plans. In other words, there is no well-defined scope and, therefore, no believable release plan.”\nAccenture appeared to be facing a £1 billion penalty payment to the government if it abandoned the program. Strangely, upon swift negotiations with the health service, and after making a penalty payment of just £63 million, Accenture duly exited the program. CSC quickly took on their contracts, tripling its involvement in the program. However, there were continuing problems with the Lorenzo software at iSoft. Meanwhile, the other two providers, BT and Fujitsu, were trying to implement American software IDX, which is not easy in a British hospital because American hospitals rely on billing for each activity and do not expect to have to handle waiting lists. In June 2005, Fujitsu dropped IDX and replaced it with another American firm, Cerner, with a software package called Millennium. BT still struggled with IDX.\nA group of experts stated: “The NHS would most likely have been better off without the National Program in terms of what is likely to be delivered and when.” \nThe program's central aim was not achieved under the contract, which was massively overpaid. For example, acute trusts cost £23 vs. an initial £8 million. The second issue concerns de-scoping. The program has dealt with its problems by reducing the scope of what is being delivered but without corresponding cost reductions. The third concern was with hiding increased costs. The late deliveries have meant no running costs for systems that have not been delivered, and the surplus cash is being used to hide the increasing cost per deployment. Fourthly, there were severe doubts about the platform's commercial judgment and skill. Very little of the initially expected system has been delivered, but the NHS has little or no commercial cover. Fujitsu's termination from the program was farcical and generated massive potential costs and liabilities. The fifth conclusion was the danger of future high costs. When the contracts are finished, inadequate provisions are made to manage the systems in the future. There were big concerns about the program’s indifference to securing clinical buy-in from users. The National Program for IT finally ended in 2011, although the enormously expensive and controversial project had been due for years to come.\n\nCase#7: The National Program for IT by the NHS","studentQuestions":["What were the main procurement and governance failures?","Why did the program become commercially unstable?","What lessons should procurement learn from this case?"],"relatedTopics":["Digitalization Paradox","Integrated Procurement Planning","TCO of a Technology Service","Incomplete Contracts","Relational Contracts","IT Procurement Contracts"],"embeddedCourseGuidance":"The national program for IT in the health service is the largest civilian computer project in the world. It was spawned in late 2001. A far-reaching vision set out a program that would supposedly transform people’s healthcare experiences. Hospital admissions and appointments would be booked online. Pharmacists would no longer struggle with GPs’ indecipherable handwriting, and drug prescriptions would be handled electronically.\nPoliticians settled on a program lasting two years and nine months, from April 2003 until December 2005. Given the extent of the proposals, that was unrealistic. \nThe market research concluded that no single player could become a prime contractor in this program. NHS soon announced that the procurement process for the program would be structured to attract global IT players. These players would not get paid until they delivered, and those not up to the mark would be replaced. In May 2003, potential bidders were given a 500-page document called a draft output-based specification and told to respond within five weeks.\nIn December 2003, the value of the contracts had already shot up to £6.2 billion from the original £2.3 billion. The time scale had tripled in length, and instead of the two years and nine months from April 2003 originally promised—the contracts were now to run for 10 years.\nFour winning bidders were appointed: BT and Fujitsu with a US software firm, IDX, and Accenture and CSC with a British software company called iSoft, whose flagship software system, Lorenzo, would be “available from early 2004.” Lorenzo caused a big headache for Accenture, which had two contracts worth around £1 billion each. Eventually, Accenture and CSC produced a confidential report in February 2006 stating that Lorenzo had “no mapping of features to release, nor detailed plans. In other words, there is no well-defined scope and, therefore, no believable release plan.”\nAccenture appeared to be facing a £1 billion penalty payment to the government if it abandoned the program. Strangely, upon swift negotiations with the health service, and after making a penalty payment of just £63 million, Accenture duly exited the program. CSC quickly took on their contracts, tripling its involvement in the program. However, there were continuing problems with the Lorenzo software at iSoft. Meanwhile, the other two providers, BT and Fujitsu, were trying to implement American software IDX, which is not easy in a British hospital because American hospitals rely on billing for each activity and do not expect to have to handle waiting lists. In June 2005, Fujitsu dropped IDX and replaced it with another American firm, Cerner, with a software package called Millennium. BT still struggled with IDX.\nA group of experts stated: “The NHS would most likely have been better off without the National Program in terms of what is likely to be delivered and when.” \nThe program's central aim was not achieved under the contract, which was massively overpaid. For example, acute trusts cost £23 vs. an initial £8 million. The second issue concerns de-scoping. The program has dealt with its problems by reducing the scope of what is being delivered but without corresponding cost reductions. The third concern was with hiding increased costs. The late deliveries have meant no running costs for systems that have not been delivered, and the surplus cash is being used to hide the increasing cost per deployment. Fourthly, there were severe doubts about the platform's commercial judgment and skill. Very little of the initially expected system has been delivered, but the NHS has little or no commercial cover. Fujitsu's termination from the program was farcical and generated massive potential costs and liabilities. The fifth conclusion was the danger of future high costs. When the contracts are finished, inadequate provisions are made to manage the systems in the future. There were big concerns about the program’s indifference to securing clinical buy-in from users. The National Program for IT finally ended in 2011, although the enormously expensive and controversial project had been due for years to come.\n\nCase #7: The National Program for IT by the NHS","source":{"caseDeck":"Training Cases (June 2026).pptx","caseSlide":8,"courseDeck":"CIPS Technology IT Procurement Foundations Slides July 2026 (With Menti and Additional Slides).pptx","courseSlide":111}},"knowledge":{"slide-036":{"slideNumber":36,"content":"Integrated \u000bProcurement Planning"},"slide-037":{"slideNumber":37,"content":"Source: ITIL® is a (registered) Trademark of AXELOS Limited.\n\nService strategy\n\nService Knowledge Management System (SKMS) \nincluding the Service Portfolio and Service Catalogue\n\nService design\n\nService transition\n\nService operation\n\nREQUIREMENTS\n\nTHE BUSINESS / CUSTOMERS\n\nOperational plans\n\nImprovement actions & plans\n\nTransition plans\n\nSKMS updated\n\nTested solutions\n\nSolution designs\n\nArchitectures\n\nStandards\n\nSDPs\n\nOperational services\n\nStrategies\n\nPolicies\n\nResource and constraints\n\nSLPs from Requirements\n\nContinual service improvement"},"slide-038":{"slideNumber":38,"content":""},"slide-040":{"slideNumber":40,"content":"When does a project end?"},"slide-041":{"slideNumber":41,"content":"Session 2 Integrated Procurement Planning"},"slide-043":{"slideNumber":43,"content":"The fundamental economic problem is scarcity. Wants are unlimited, and resources are finite, so choices must be made. Resources must be used and distributed optimally. This gives rise to opportunity cost. The opportunity cost of a choice is the value of the next best alternative forgone. \nIn 2016, British Airways outsourced its UK-based IT staff (over 700 people) as part of its business strategy. \nThis outsourcing strategy allegedly resulted in a 2017 global flight disruption that affected 75,000 passengers and caused a nearly £150 million loss.\nPreviously, BA claimed a 25% reduction in its IT operational costs. However, due to some simple considerations, it’s unclear if the outsourcing risk exposure has been accounted for in the business case. \nA typical large company's IT budget (over $1 billion in revenues) is 2% of revenues, translating into approximately £210 million for BA in 2016. Let's assume 50% of that is related to infrastructure operations, maintenance, support, security, etc. A 25% saving corresponds to just £27.5 million vs. the 2017 crisis cost of £150 million.\nLet's assume a business case as follows.\n\nOpportunity cost & TCO (British Airways IT Outsourcing)\n\nWhat would you recommend - outsourcing or insourcing?\nWhat could be the opportunity cost?\n\n2017 risk exposure of £150 million makes this business case break even.  \nThe average revenue per employee in the airline industry in 2016 was £350,000 * 700 * 5 years = £1,225 billion.\n\nWith a profit margin of 5.1% in the airline industry in 2016, £1 savings = £ 19.61 in revenue. The opportunity cost is £1,225 billion / £19.61 = £62.5 million."},"slide-083":{"slideNumber":83,"content":"Sourcing of IT \u000bServices"},"slide-084":{"slideNumber":84,"content":"Session 4 Sourcing of IT Services\n\nManaged Services\n\nMobilization\n\nOccasional covering of resource gaps\n\nOutsourcing of complete teams to deliver a project or a simple business process\n\nInfrastructure-centric outsourcing of highly complex business processes or critical services\n\nQuick\n\nSlow\n\nSlowest\n\nContract\n\nHighest\n\nNormal\n\nLow\n\nHighest\n\nNormal\n\nHigh\n\nIT Outsourcing\n\nStaff Augmentation\n\nAs-a-Service\n\nControl\n\nRate card / T&M\n\nProject fee or retainer\n\nSLA-based long-term contract with incentives, possibly with the transfer of skills\n\nApplication-centric automated standard tasks in the cloud\n\nImmediate\n\nLowest\n\nLowest\n\nBoiler-plate subscription agreement\n\nCost\n\nUse case\n\nSource of the competitive advantage\nConfidential information and customer data\nCorporate strategy and decision-making.\nRisk management and compliance \nContract negotiation and key legal reviews\n\nCustomer & stakeholder relationships\nHigh-value customer management \nR&D and product design\nCritical data analytics\nPerformance governance (incl. outsourcing management)\n\nWhat critical activities would you NOT outsource?"},"slide-085":{"slideNumber":85,"content":"Session 4 Sourcing of IT Services\n\nService category\n\nInsourced\n\nManaged Services\n\nStaff Augmentation\n\nSelective Sourcing\n\naaS\n\nTypical observations\n\nIT Strategy, Governance & \nStakeholder Management\n\nMission-critical roles essential to organizations ability to delivery quality IT service will always be insourced\n\nIT Support & End-User \u000bEnablement\n\nCommodity services available in market at greater maturity than IT requirements are typically outsourced\n\nEnterprise & Solution \nArchitecture\n\nEnterprise Architecture is retained as a mission-critical strategic function for the IT organization\n\nApplication Management\nEnterprise Applications\n\nValue-add corporate platforms and apps are insource, automation & cloud adoption used for outsource\n\nApplication Management\nBusiness Applications\n\nRetain critical business skills and minimal team for agility in development, outsource day-to-day management\n\nInfrastructure Operations\n\nCommodity services available in market at greater maturity than IT requirements are typically outsourced\n\nProject Execution\n\nRetain PMO & PM team for business agility and expertise, leverage partners for on-demand capacity\n\nInnovation Management\n\nRetain core capability and leverage ecosystem for idea generation and agile execution"},"slide-096":{"slideNumber":96,"content":""},"slide-097":{"slideNumber":97,"content":"Session 5 Source-to-Contract\n\nComply \u000bLevers\n\nDemand\u000bLevers\n\nSource\u000bLevers\n\nFulfil\u000bLevers\n\nManage\u000bLevers\n\nProcess\u000bLevers\n\nSpend \ngovernance\n\nEliminate \ndemand\n\nType of sourcing event\n\nBundle/unbundle\n\nDemand / supply balancing\n\nP2P control\n\nPolicy compliance\n\nReview quantity\n\nCycle time\n\nSupplier rationalization\n\nOrdering\n\nVariations\n\nReduce frequency\n\nPrioritize projects\n\nVolume or time commitment\n\nPhysical delivery\n\nRisk\n\nStandardize specs\n\nBuying channels\n\nPricing mechanism\n\nStorage and distribution\n\nPerformance\n\nReduce portfolio\n\nSource from OEM\n\nPerformance or benefit incentive\n\nContract compliance\n\nValue delivery\n\nConsider alternatives\n\nRisk allocation\n\nRepair and replacement\n\nAdditional benefits (revenue, marketing, etc)\n\nEncourage reuse\n\nPayment terms\n\nTransaction cost\n\nInitiate a sourcing project\n\nIdentify business needs and study \u000bthe market\n\nSpecify requirements\n\nDefine a sourcing strategy\n\nSelect a supplier and award a contract\n\nManage a contract and supplier relationship\n\nSourcing \nvalue levers"},"slide-101":{"slideNumber":101,"content":"Ub\n\nUa\n\nUb\n\nUa\n\nDistributive negotiations \n(fixed pie, win-lose, zero-sum games)\n\nIntegrative negotiations \n(win-win, cooperative games)\n\nU1\n\nU2\n\nU3\n\nU4\n\nimprovement\n\nconcession\n\nClaiming value\n\nCreating value\n\nTypes of Negotiation\n\nSession 5 Source-to-Contract\n\nKey Tactics in Cooperative Games:\nInterest Exploration: Understanding each party's underlying interests rather than focusing on positions.\nSharing Information: Revealing relevant information to find common ground and identify creative solutions.\nBuilding Trust: Encouraging transparency and openness to facilitate more effective problem-solving.\nJoint Problem-Solving: Discussing solutions benefiting both parties, like finding ways to cut costs without compromising quality.\nCreating Multiple Options: Propose a variety of possible outcomes and then evaluate them together to choose the best one.\n\nKey Tactics in Zero-Sum Games:\nAggressive Anchoring: Start with an extreme initial offer to influence the negotiation range.\nClaiming Value: Focus on maximizing your share of the fixed pie rather than expanding it.\nWithholding Information: Only reveal information that strengthens your position.\nUsing Leverage: Emphasize your power, options, and alternatives to push for a better outcome.\n\nWhat quadrants of the Kraljic matrix does each type of negotiation fit?"},"slide-104":{"slideNumber":104,"content":"Scenario Overview:\nA buyer invests in specialized equipment to increase profits (P). The supplier may raise prices (R), leading to different outcomes depending on whether the buyer continues with the supplier or switches.\nKey Variables:\n• P = increased profits\n• V = investment cost\n• R = price increase by supplier\n• B = loss of business for supplier\nScenario 1: Buyer Invests; Supplier Does Not Raise Prices\n• Buyer Payoff: P - V\n• Supplier Payoff: £0\nOutcome: Positive for Buyer if P > V; Neutral for Supplier\nScenario 2: Supplier Raises Prices\n• Buyer Payoff: P – V - R\n• Supplier Payoff: R\nOutcome: Positive if P-V-R > £0; Negative otherwise\nScenario 3: Buyer Switches Suppliers After Price Increase\n• Buyer Payoff: P-V-R-(P-R)=  -V \n• Supplier Payoff: R – B \nOutcome: Negative for Both Buyer and Supplier. DON'T INVEST.\n\n• In Scenario 3, both parties suffer losses, showing the importance of contract design and trust.\n\nWhat are the possible solutions to overcome the hold-up problem?\n\nVertical integration (insourcing)\nLong-term contracts fixing critical (red-flag) terms\nRevenue share"},"slide-105":{"slideNumber":105,"content":"Session 5 Source-to-Contract\n\nTransaction spectrum\n\nCompany size\n\nRelational contracts\n\nFormal contracts\n\nSmall\n\nCorporate\n\nRelational\n\nDiscrete\n\nFirm fixed price\n\nCost plus\n\nTarget price\n\nTime and material\n\n“ … contractual relations, not discrete contract, always have and will always be the dominant form of exchange behavior in the society. ” (Macneil 1985)"},"slide-106":{"slideNumber":106,"content":"Session 5 Source-to-Contract"},"slide-110":{"slideNumber":110,"content":""},"slide-111":{"slideNumber":111,"content":"The national program for IT in the health service is the largest civilian computer project in the world. It was spawned in late 2001. A far-reaching vision set out a program that would supposedly transform people’s healthcare experiences. Hospital admissions and appointments would be booked online. Pharmacists would no longer struggle with GPs’ indecipherable handwriting, and drug prescriptions would be handled electronically.\nPoliticians settled on a program lasting two years and nine months, from April 2003 until December 2005. Given the extent of the proposals, that was unrealistic. \nThe market research concluded that no single player could become a prime contractor in this program. NHS soon announced that the procurement process for the program would be structured to attract global IT players. These players would not get paid until they delivered, and those not up to the mark would be replaced. In May 2003, potential bidders were given a 500-page document called a draft output-based specification and told to respond within five weeks.\nIn December 2003, the value of the contracts had already shot up to £6.2 billion from the original £2.3 billion. The time scale had tripled in length, and instead of the two years and nine months from April 2003 originally promised—the contracts were now to run for 10 years.\nFour winning bidders were appointed: BT and Fujitsu with a US software firm, IDX, and Accenture and CSC with a British software company called iSoft, whose flagship software system, Lorenzo, would be “available from early 2004.” Lorenzo caused a big headache for Accenture, which had two contracts worth around £1 billion each. Eventually, Accenture and CSC produced a confidential report in February 2006 stating that Lorenzo had “no mapping of features to release, nor detailed plans. In other words, there is no well-defined scope and, therefore, no believable release plan.”\nAccenture appeared to be facing a £1 billion penalty payment to the government if it abandoned the program. Strangely, upon swift negotiations with the health service, and after making a penalty payment of just £63 million, Accenture duly exited the program. CSC quickly took on their contracts, tripling its involvement in the program. However, there were continuing problems with the Lorenzo software at iSoft. Meanwhile, the other two providers, BT and Fujitsu, were trying to implement American software IDX, which is not easy in a British hospital because American hospitals rely on billing for each activity and do not expect to have to handle waiting lists. In June 2005, Fujitsu dropped IDX and replaced it with another American firm, Cerner, with a software package called Millennium. BT still struggled with IDX.\nA group of experts stated: “The NHS would most likely have been better off without the National Program in terms of what is likely to be delivered and when.” \nThe program's central aim was not achieved under the contract, which was massively overpaid. For example, acute trusts cost £23 vs. an initial £8 million. The second issue concerns de-scoping. The program has dealt with its problems by reducing the scope of what is being delivered but without corresponding cost reductions. The third concern was with hiding increased costs. The late deliveries have meant no running costs for systems that have not been delivered, and the surplus cash is being used to hide the increasing cost per deployment. Fourthly, there were severe doubts about the platform's commercial judgment and skill. Very little of the initially expected system has been delivered, but the NHS has little or no commercial cover. Fujitsu's termination from the program was farcical and generated massive potential costs and liabilities. The fifth conclusion was the danger of future high costs. When the contracts are finished, inadequate provisions are made to manage the systems in the future. There were big concerns about the program’s indifference to securing clinical buy-in from users. The National Program for IT finally ended in 2011, although the enormously expensive and controversial project had been due for years to come.\n\nCase #7: The National Program for IT by the NHS"}}}};
const ALLOWED_ORIGINS = ['https://www.goodspending.com','https://ukrsedo.github.io','http://localhost:8000','http://127.0.0.1:8000'];
const RESULT_SCHEMA={type:'object',additionalProperties:false,properties:{score:{type:'integer',minimum:0,maximum:5},correctPoints:{type:'array',items:{type:'string'},minItems:1,maxItems:6},missingPoint:{type:'string'},strongerAnswer:{type:'array',items:{type:'string'},minItems:3,maxItems:5},challengeRequired:{type:'boolean'},challengeQuestions:{type:'array',items:{type:'string'},maxItems:2}},required:['score','correctPoints','missingPoint','strongerAnswer','challengeRequired','challengeQuestions']};
export default {async fetch(request,env){const origin=request.headers.get('Origin')||'';const headers={'Access-Control-Allow-Origin':ALLOWED_ORIGINS.includes(origin)?origin:ALLOWED_ORIGINS[0],'Access-Control-Allow-Methods':'POST,OPTIONS','Access-Control-Allow-Headers':'Content-Type','Vary':'Origin'};if(request.method==='OPTIONS')return new Response(null,{status:204,headers});const url=new URL(request.url);if(url.pathname!=='/assess-case'||request.method!=='POST')return json({message:'Not found.'},404,headers);if(!env.OPENAI_API_KEY)return json({message:'OPENAI_API_KEY is not configured.'},500,headers);try{const body=await request.json();const item=CASE_DATA[body.caseId];if(!item)return json({message:'Unknown case ID.'},400,headers);const isChallenge=Array.isArray(body.challengeAnswers)&&body.challengeAnswers.length>0;const prompt=buildPrompt(item,body,isChallenge);const api=await fetch('https://api.openai.com/v1/responses',{method:'POST',headers:{'Authorization':`Bearer ${env.OPENAI_API_KEY}`,'Content-Type':'application/json'},body:JSON.stringify({model:env.OPENAI_MODEL||'gpt-5.6-luna',store:false,input:[{role:'system',content:[{type:'input_text',text:SYSTEM}]},{role:'user',content:[{type:'input_text',text:prompt}]}],text:{format:{type:'json_schema',name:'case_assessment',strict:true,schema:RESULT_SCHEMA}}})});const raw=await api.json();if(!api.ok)return json({message:raw?.error?.message||`OpenAI request failed (${api.status}).`},502,headers);const text=raw.output_text||extractText(raw);if(!text)return json({message:'The assessment model returned no usable result.'},502,headers);const result=JSON.parse(text);return json(result,200,headers)}catch(e){return json({message:e.message||'Assessment failed.'},500,headers)}}};
const SYSTEM=`You assess answers for the IT Procurement Foundations course. Use only the supplied case, embedded course guidance, and course slide extracts. Do not use outside knowledge. Accept concise bullet answers and different wording when reasoning is correct. Score the complete case from 0 to 5. Do not require essays. Output only the required JSON.\n\nScoring guide:\n5 = all material questions correctly addressed with sound course-based reasoning.\n4 = core answer correct with one secondary omission.\n3 = partially correct but an important concept is missing.\n2 = limited understanding with major gaps.\n1 = minimal relevant reasoning.\n0 = blank, irrelevant, or fundamentally incorrect.\n\nChallenge questions: use only when the initial answer is weak but shows enough understanding to improve through one or two targeted questions. After challenge answers are supplied, provide the final score and set challengeRequired to false. The stronger answer must contain 3-5 concise bullets. The missingPoint must identify the single most important gap, or state that no material point is missing for a score of 5.`;
function buildPrompt(item,body,isChallenge){const c=item.case;const knowledge=Object.entries(item.knowledge).map(([id,v])=>`[${id}]\n${typeof v==='string'?v:JSON.stringify(v)}`).join('\n\n');const initial=Array.isArray(body.answers)?body.answers:[];return `CASE ID: ${c.caseId}\nTITLE: ${c.title}\n\nAUTHORITATIVE CASE CONTENT:\n${c.authoritativeCaseContent}\n\nQUESTIONS:\n${c.studentQuestions.map((q,i)=>`${i+1}. ${q}`).join('\n')}\n\nEMBEDDED COURSE GUIDANCE:\n${c.embeddedCourseGuidance||'None'}\n\nRELEVANT COURSE MATERIAL:\n${knowledge}\n\n${isChallenge?`PREVIOUS ASSESSMENT:\n${JSON.stringify(body.previousAssessment||{})}\n\nCHALLENGE ANSWERS:\n${body.challengeAnswers.map((a,i)=>`${i+1}. ${a}`).join('\n')}`:`STUDENT ANSWERS:\n${initial.map((a,i)=>`${i+1}. ${a}`).join('\n')}`}\n\nAssess only against the supplied material.`}
function extractText(r){for(const o of r.output||[])for(const c of o.content||[])if(c.type==='output_text'&&c.text)return c.text;return ''}
function json(body,status,headers){return new Response(JSON.stringify(body),{status,headers:{...headers,'Content-Type':'application/json'}})}
